# =============================================================================
# Two groups. The split matters when you build the production file.
#
#   A. Secrets and per-environment URLs  -> go into ENV_API_BASE64
#   B. Runtime topology (at the bottom)  -> LOCAL DEV ONLY; the deploy sets these
#
# To make a production file: copy this, fill in group A, and DELETE group B.
# `pnpm env:base64 <env>` warns if you leave group B in.
# =============================================================================

# --- A. Goes into ENV_API_BASE64 ---------------------------------------------
# Everything from here down to group B, including the Mail and Google blocks.

# Dev and production both publish Postgres on 5004, so these two URLs differ only
# by host and password. In production use 127.0.0.1, never `localhost` — it can
# resolve to ::1, where the loopback-bound container is not listening.
#
#   dev:   postgresql://bookie:bookie@localhost:5004/bookie?schema=public
#   prod:  postgresql://bookie:<hex-password>@127.0.0.1:5004/bookie?schema=public
#
# See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://bookie:bookie@localhost:5004/bookie?schema=public"
# Generate with `openssl rand -hex 64` — hex rather than base64 so the value has
# no `/ + =` for systemd's EnvironmentFile parser or a shell to misread.
#
# It signs four things: the 7-day session JWT (lib/session.ts), the Google OAuth
# state+PKCE cookie (lib/oauth-state.ts), the pending-Google-account cookie
# (routes/identity-google.ts), and the HMAC in a guest booking's manage link
# (lib/token.ts).
#
# Rotating it therefore signs every user out, kills any in-flight Google sign-in,
# and breaks every outstanding booking-manage link. Email-verification and
# password-reset links are NOT affected — those are random tokens stored hashed
# in the database, not signed with this.
JWT_SECRET="change-me-in-production-use-long-random-string"
# The exact origin serving the web app. requireSameOrigin compares against it
# character for character, so it must equal the NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL repository
# variable — no trailing slash.
CORS_ORIGIN="http://localhost:7004"

# --- Admin -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who may reach /admin/* — the review-moderation queue. Comma-separated identity
# emails, matched case-insensitively against the signed-in user's own address.
#
# Not a role and not a column: SessionPayload carries only `consumer | provider`,
# so an admin signs in with whichever account they already have and is recognised
# by this list. Membership is re-read from the User row on every request, so
# removing someone here takes effect on their next request rather than when their
# 7-day cookie expires.
#
# Leave EMPTY in local dev unless you are working on moderation. An empty list
# admits NOBODY, and /admin/* answers 404 — not 403 — to everyone it admits, so
# the surface does not announce itself to a signed-in non-admin.
ADMIN_EMAILS=""

# --- Mail engine -------------------------------------------------------------
# Base URL of the external mail engine. No trailing slash needed; config strips one.
MAIL_API_URL="https://api-mail-engine.mnavasardian.com"
# Sent as the `x-api-key` header. Read only by src/lib/mail.ts, never logged.
# Leave EMPTY in local dev: sends are skipped, the contact form still stores the
# message, and the email-verification link still prints to the API console.
MAIL_API_KEY=""
# Identifies this app to the engine; must match the id registered there.
MAIL_APP_ID="bookie"

# --- Google OAuth ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Creating the credential
# -----------------------
# 1. Google Cloud console > select (or create) the project.
# 2. APIs & Services > OAuth consent screen. Pick "External" unless this is a
#    Workspace-only deployment. Fill in app name, support email and developer
#    email; add the scopes `.../auth/userinfo.email` and
#    `.../auth/userinfo.profile` — those are the two src/lib/google-oauth.ts
#    requests ('profile', 'email'), and nothing else is needed.
#    While the screen is in "Testing", only accounts listed under Test users can
#    sign in. Publish it before real users arrive.
# 3. APIs & Services > Credentials > Create credentials > OAuth client ID,
#    Application type: "Web application".
# 4. Fill in the two URI lists exactly as below, then copy the client ID and
#    secret into the two variables further down.
#
# Authorized JavaScript origins
# -----------------------------
#   LEAVE THIS EMPTY. It is not used by this app.
#
# That field only matters when the *browser* talks to Google directly — Google
# Identity Services, One Tap, or the implicit flow — because those read the
# origin to decide whether to answer a cross-origin request. Bookie does none of
# that: GoogleButton does `window.location.href = …` (a top-level navigation to
# our own API), and the API then performs the authorization-code exchange
# server-to-server with the client secret. No Google JavaScript SDK is ever
# loaded in the page, so there is no origin for Google to authorize.
#
# Adding the web origin anyway is harmless, but it is not what makes sign-in
# work — the redirect URI below is. If Google One Tap is ever introduced, this
# field becomes required AND the "no ID-token verification needed" reasoning in
# src/lib/google-oauth.ts stops holding; read that file's header first.
#
# Authorized redirect URIs
# ------------------------
# Add one entry per environment, matched character for character — scheme, host,
# port, path, no trailing slash. A mismatch fails at Google with redirect_uri_mismatch
# before our code is ever reached.
#
#   dev:   http://localhost:9004/identity/google/callback
#   prod:  https://api.<domain>/identity/google/callback
#
# Note the port and host: :9004 is the API, NOT the web app on :7004, and in
# production it is the `api.` subdomain, not the site itself. The session cookie
# is host-only on the API origin, so the callback has to land on the API to be
# able to set it. Pointing this at the web app is the single most common way to
# get a flow that completes at Google and then drops the user signed-out.
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET is read only by src/lib/google-oauth.ts and is never
# logged, returned, or put in an error.
#
# Leave BOTH empty in local dev: /health reports google:false, the web app hides
# the Google button, and /identity/google redirects back with
# ?error=google_unavailable instead of throwing.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""
# Must equal one of the Authorized redirect URIs registered above, exactly.
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI="http://localhost:9004/identity/google/callback"

# --- B. LOCAL DEV ONLY — leave these out of ENV_API_BASE64 -------------------
#
# These four describe where the process runs, not what it is allowed to do, and
# the deploy workflow sets all four from its own inputs so they cannot drift from
# the systemd units and the nginx server blocks that have to agree with them.
# Anything you put here is stripped and rewritten on the way to the server.
#
# The reason it works this way is that three of the four fail SILENTLY:
#
#   NODE_ENV    left at `development` -> the session cookie loses `Secure`, and
#               requireSameOrigin stops rejecting requests that carry neither
#               Origin nor Referer. The app still appears to work.
#   UPLOAD_DIR  left at `./uploads`   -> resolves inside the release directory,
#               which release rotation deletes: every uploaded image disappears
#               on the fourth deploy.
#   HOST        left unset            -> the API binds every interface instead of
#               loopback, so it is reachable without going through nginx.
#   PORT        changed               -> nginx proxies to a port nothing is
#               listening on. This one at least fails loudly, at the health check.

# dev only — production: the deploy sets 9004, matching the nginx proxy_pass
# and the systemd unit.
PORT=9004

# dev only — production: the deploy sets 127.0.0.1 so the API is reachable only
# through nginx. Unset in dev binds every interface, which is what lets a phone
# on the same Wi-Fi reach the dev server.
# HOST=127.0.0.1

# dev only — production: the deploy sets $APP_DIR/shared/uploads, outside the
# releases and matching the nginx alias.
# Where multer writes uploads and express.static serves /uploads from. It is a
# variable at all because the default — `../uploads` relative to config.js —
# resolves inside the release once compiled, and release rotation deletes that.
UPLOAD_DIR="./uploads"

# dev only — production: the deploy sets `production`, which is what turns on the
# `Secure` session cookie and requireSameOrigin's strict mode.
NODE_ENV=development
